;;; A guard on a variable no premise before it binds reads nothing, in
;;; silence — M1e2 **W24**, register row `W24/guard-before-binder`
;;; (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`), banked by S1e2.2.24 (2026-11-05) from its
;;; step 1's probe `build/s1e2.2.24/shapes/g-order-rule.ein` (the program
;;; unchanged), ruled by the user on 2026-11-05 (Q-M1e2.18 (ii)(a)).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Four facts of `p`, and two rules that write their guard before the
;;; premise that binds its variable: `(and (eq ?x a) (p ?x))` and
;;; `(and (neq ?x a) (p ?x))`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A guard is judged where it is written, and compares the values the
;;; premises before it bind. The user ruled that a guard on a variable no
;;; premise before it binds is refused at load, naming the variable, with a
;;; line in Ein's spelling naming the rule and the clause. Every command that
;;; loads the program exits 1 (`ein test`: 2).
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered, and why it does not any more ─────────────
;;;
;;; Until S1e2.2.24 the program loaded, and each guard compared `?x`, not yet
;;; bound, as a null, equal to no value: `e` never fired, and `n` fired for
;;; every `?x`, `a` included. Root derived `(hit-neq a)`, `(hit-neq b)`,
;;; `(hit-neq 1)` and `(hit-neq (f a))` and no `hit-eq`, exit 0, with no word
;;; of the order (`ein saturate --dump FILE` and `ein solve FILE` at
;;; `f8c10730`, release and debug). Since then the loader refuses it: *kb
;;; load error: rule 'e': its :match writes (eq ?x a), and no premise before
;;; it binds ?x — a guard compares two values, each a variable a premise
;;; before it binds, a name, a string or an integer*, and the same line for
;;; `n`.

(relation p Node) (relation q Node) (relation f Node) (relation hit-eq Node) (relation hit-neq Node)
(p a) (p b) (p 1) (p (f a)) (q a)
(rule e () :match (and (eq ?x a) (p ?x)) :assert (hit-eq ?x))
(rule n () :match (and (neq ?x a) (p ?x)) :assert (hit-neq ?x))
